{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact R3a head 07cc88520d3e46434c72972752d87505750a65a4 on builder/authority-key-admission-r3a-20261109, tree cce2775d, all eight artifacts, for request 31f98f33 under promise cf19eb3a and review request e85e6cd8. A signer key now takes one of the run's 4,096 places only after a checked observation gives it current standing. Unknown keys, failed reads and missing answers take no place and evict nothing. The head implements rulings dbf97956 and 19647eda as written.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- Admission order. `read` no longer reserves before Membership. After the awaited read it rereads the clock and judges the observation that counts: the kept or denied revocation if one exists, otherwise the arrival. Only `current` reserves a place. The size check and `keys.add` run with no await between them, so two final arrivals cannot both pass 4,096. A once-only grant takes a place but is not kept.\n- What each answer leaves behind. An `unknown` answer is stored nowhere. A revoked answer goes to `kept` when the key is admitted, so the denial stays sticky for the run. Otherwise it goes to the `denied` FIFO. An update keeps the key's FIFO position, and only a new distinct revoked key evicts.\n- Eviction. Each eviction advances `generation`. A read whose generation moved while it waited returns null. `held()` returns null for a handle made before the move, and so does `sealed()`. In turn.ts, `#judged` runs the judgment, `#seal` and `verdict.sealed` with no await, so one commit's `held()` and `sealed()` always see the same generation. The guard in `sealed()` is defensive only.\n- The structures. `kept` gains active observations only for admitted keys. `retained` gains a key only through `sealed()` after `held()` granted `current`. `denied` holds at most 4,096 keys. `cacheCounts()` returns four sizes and no key data, and no route exposes it.\n- The seal. I recomputed authority.ts outside the builder's worktree: 1ff72892…, equal to its literal. ports.ts changes only its comment.\n- The threat model. The paragraph states the standing-only charge, the FIFO size, the changed guarantee for never-admitted keys and the unbounded `Further.read` map that request 22b57b9e owns. Its witness list follows the renamed and added cases.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main 86179b0c is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the eight paths that its eight artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes: 8 checked, 0 flagged. No dependency, lockfile, Wrangler, definition, pin, recorded-format or migration file changes.\n- The tests. The old R3 capacity case asserted a refusal before reading. The ruling replaces that rule, so a case with the new rule replaces it. The new case also resolves the two final arrivals in reverse order. The newcomer case makes 4,100 unknown, failed or missing reads, then gives a newly joined member standing, with every count at zero after the flood. The eviction case holds key 0's active answer in flight while 4,096 later revocations push key 0 out. The five existing authority cases keep their assertions.\n- The controls. Restoring the reservation before reading fails the newcomer case with \"Expected current standing, got limit\". Removing `generation++` fails the eviction case because the old active answer grants. Both owners exit 1, and the source is restored. The first wrapper exited 1 before any fault ran and stays recorded.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at 62d33377: \"exit 0\". Node 144, Counting 56, Plain 4,028 plus one skip, and Native 1,126 plus two skips. After the gate, only the note and the gate manifest change. Before the gate, after the focused checkpoint de03d2b3, only the note and the pre-gate manifest change.\n- The evidence. The two manifests hash to the cited values, bbe20b50… and 2a2a29fd…. Their 40 files match their sizes and digests and are read-only. My credential scan matched only `github_pat_` inside the builder's own scan pattern in seal-driver.py, not a credential.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The bound now counts what it means to count, and the port still makes Membership the source of truth. All four structures stay private to one run.\n\nSecurity: no finding. A caller without real standing cannot take a place, evict a denial or lock out a newcomer. A never-admitted revoked key that leaves the FIFO is refused again by a fresh Membership read.\n\nOne limit holds by design, under ruling dbf97956, and the threat model states it only in part: eviction invalidates every handle and read in flight, not only those of the evicted key. The eviction case asserts that admitted key 5000's outstanding handle returns null, and a read that causes an eviction returns null for itself. Once the FIFO is full, someone who holds the private keys of more than 4,096 really revoked keys can make concurrent acts of other members unavailable, though not refused, one read at a time. I am asking the planner separately whether to state this or narrow it.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two notes:\n- The generation guard in `sealed()` cannot fire after `held()` granted, because the commit is synchronous.\n- `retained` stays inside the admitted set only because a read judged `current` before its commit. If a commit judges `current` after a read judged `unauthorized`, a key could enter `retained` without a place. That needs the incarnation to become fixed between the read and the commit, which happens once in a scope's life.\n\nThe planner deploys after landing. No setting changes. This approval authorizes no deployment and closes no live acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7fa31db01104f9aa99de27519c9654ee0b8c854","binding":"assigned","head":"07cc88520d3e46434c72972752d87505750a65a4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c031cf968d7aeb6e87d0fd949d861f8372419291\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cf19eb3ad7c503a37e6900c0c65869eb410845a2\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c031cf968d7aeb6e87d0fd949d861f8372419291","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}